{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2251","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2251","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2251\",\"parent_revision\":\"a69a2195089a45df54a23cd1841e1e09ee8463ae\",\"root_revision\":\"c7c565fc1798745cb260e02adebef20da1cb0b069209292baa9bfbea853b28ff\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **code-review: eval/benchmark suite with structured pass/fail scenarios for code-review skills** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md \u2014 pattern: 49-scenario evals.json benchmark suite\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-10\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-17)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS**\\n\\n| Input | Status | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Functional intent | AVAILABLE | Eval suite for code-reviewer agent and per-stack skills with structured pass/fail scenarios |\\n| Scope boundaries | AVAILABLE | Target: `plugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/evals/evals.json` + runner script |\\n| Existing eval precedent | AVAILABLE | `.agents/skills/copy-editing/evals/evals.json` confirmed at path (id, prompt, expected_output, assertions, files schema) |\\n| Code-reviewer interface | AVAILABLE | `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 7 universal quality dimensions documented |\\n| Per-stack skill inventory | AVAILABLE | 7 skills confirmed: python, typescript, nodejs, web, cli, llm, claude-skills |\\n| brooks-lint eval pattern | AVAILABLE | `research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md` \u2014 49 scenarios, R1\u2013R6, T1\u2013T6, false-positive guards |\\n| Iron Law finding format | DERIVABLE | Defined by #2248 (Iron Law item); code-review-shared skill (#2250) not yet created \u2014 eval `expected_findings` schema depends on final format |\\n| Eval runner invocation method | MISSING | How `run_code_review_evals.py` calls the agent (Claude CLI subprocess, deterministic rule-engine, MCP) is undefined \u2014 required before implementation |\\n| Scenario count distribution | PARTIAL | Item states \\\"at least 10 total\\\"; per-dimension and per-guard distribution unspecified |\\n\\n**Missing inputs \u2014 affect execution only, not planning:**\\n1. Runner invocation method \u2014 affects `run_code_review_evals.py` design only\\n2. Whether evals block on #2250 landing first (directory target dependency)\\n\\n**Unblock actions:**\\n- Human decision on runner invocation method (see Questions for Human)\\n- Clarify whether evals directory can be created before #2250 is done\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 The code-reviewer is the only S6 quality gate in every SAM feature delivery. Accuracy regressions are silent without an eval baseline. The copy-editing precedent already establishes the pattern; this is adaptation work, not pioneering. Blocked only by one design decision (runner invocation).\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Any future refactoring of code-review skill rules \u2014 without this, accuracy cannot be measured before or after a change\\n- Bottleneck: The code-reviewer is the S6 gate for every SAM implementation; silent accuracy regressions propagate into every delivery\\n- Indirect dependency: #2248 (Iron Law) and #2250 (code-review-shared) affect the finding format the `expected_findings` schema should match\\n- #2253 (Health Score) needs an eval baseline as calibration input for fail-below thresholds\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Establishes an observable accuracy baseline for the code-reviewer (analogous to brooks-lint's 94% vs 16% signal from `research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md`)\\n- Prevents skill rule edits from silently degrading review quality \u2014 any change to a `code-review-{stack}/SKILL.md` can be run against the suite before merging\\n- Provides a repeatable regression harness that can run before any code-review skill change is committed\\n- Documents what the code-reviewer is expected to detect via the scenario corpus \u2014 serves as living specification for the agent's behavior\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen the code-reviewer agent and its per-stack skills are functioning correctly:\\n\\n- A developer runs a single command and receives a structured pass/fail report showing which eval scenarios the reviewer handles correctly\\n- Skill rule changes that degrade review accuracy produce a measurable drop in the reported pass rate\\n- False-positive guard scenarios confirm the reviewer does not flag code that should not be flagged\\n- The baseline pass rate is visible as a number that can be compared across commits\\n- Reviewers loading `dh:code-review-python` (or any stack skill) produce findings that include the required structured dimensions and finding format\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nTarget file layout:\\n\\n```text\\nplugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/\\n  evals/\\n    evals.json          \u2014 scenario definitions\\nplugins/development-harness/scripts/\\n  run_code_review_evals.py  \u2014 invokes code-reviewer per scenario, computes pass rate\\n```\\n\\n`evals.json` schema (modelled on `.agents/skills/copy-editing/evals/evals.json`):\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"skill_name\\\": \\\"code-review\\\",\\n  \\\"evals\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"<code snippet or description of files under review>\\\",\\n      \\\"expected_findings\\\": [\\\"<dimension.pattern>\\\"],\\n      \\\"false_positive_guards\\\": [\\\"<pattern the reviewer must NOT flag>\\\"],\\n      \\\"assertions\\\": [\\\"<verifiable claim about the produced report>\\\"]\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nMinimum scenario distribution (at least 10 total):\\n- 1 scenario per universal quality dimension (7: Security, Correctness, Tests, API Contract, Naming, Error Handling, Performance)\\n- At least 1 false-positive guard scenario (non-empty `false_positive_guards`, correct code that must not be flagged)\\n- At least 1 tradeoff edge case scenario\\n- At least 1 stack-specific scenario (Python or TypeScript)\\n\\nRunner script computes pass/fail per scenario and reports overall pass rate. Exits non-zero when any blocking assertion fails.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `test -f plugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/evals/evals.json` exits 0\\n2. `uv run python -c \\\"import json; d = json.load(open('plugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/evals/evals.json')); assert len(d['evals']) >= 10\\\"` exits 0\\n3. Each eval entry has `id`, `expected_findings`, `false_positive_guards`, and `assertions` fields \u2014 validated by runner script schema check on startup\\n4. At least 1 scenario exists for each of the 7 universal quality dimensions (Security, Correctness, Tests, API Contract, Naming, Error Handling, Performance)\\n5. At least 1 false-positive guard scenario exists \u2014 a scenario with non-empty `false_positive_guards` covering code that must NOT be flagged\\n6. `uv run plugins/development-harness/scripts/run_code_review_evals.py` runs without error and prints a baseline pass rate percentage\\n7. The runner exits non-zero when any scenario with a blocking assertion fails\\n8. The schema is consistent with the existing precedent at `.agents/skills/copy-editing/evals/evals.json` (same top-level shape: `skill_name`, `evals` array)\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- How should the runner invoke the code-reviewer? Options: (a) `claude -p` subprocess per scenario (real LLM \u2014 slow, costs tokens, but tests the full agent), (b) a deterministic rule-engine that applies skill patterns without an LLM call (fast, CI-safe, but not testing the actual agent behavior), (c) another approach. Cost and CI runtime differ significantly between options.\\n- Should the evals block on #2250 (code-review-shared skill) being created first, or should the `evals/` directory be created independently and the skill directory added later?\\n- Should false-positive guard scenarios count toward the overall pass rate, or be a separate guard check that can fail independently?\\n- Is there a target pass rate threshold below which a skill change should be blocked (e.g., fail if pass rate drops below 80%)?\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|---|---|\\n| Eval precedent | `/home/user/claude_skills/.agents/skills/copy-editing/evals/evals.json` \u2014 schema reference (id, prompt, expected_output, assertions, files) |\\n| Agent | `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 defines 7 universal quality dimensions, output format, and verdict logic |\\n| Skill | `dh:code-review-python` \u2014 stack-specific rules; use for Python scenario authoring |\\n| Skill | `dh:code-review-typescript` \u2014 stack-specific rules; use for TypeScript scenario authoring |\\n| Research | `research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md` \u2014 49-scenario eval pattern, R1\u2013R6 / T1\u2013T6 risk taxonomy, false-positive guard discipline, Iron Law structure |\\n| Related item | #2248 \u2014 Iron Law four-part finding structure (Symptom\u2192Source\u2192Consequence\u2192Remedy); affects `expected_findings` schema design |\\n| Related item | #2250 \u2014 code-review-shared skill (destination directory for `evals/`) |\\n| Related item | #2252 \u2014 Auto-scope detection (affects how scenarios specify files under review) |\\n| Related item | #2253 \u2014 Health Score (this eval suite is the calibration baseline for fail-below thresholds) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: #2250 (code-review-shared skill) \u2014 the `evals/` directory lives under this skill; evals can be authored before the skill is complete but the target directory path depends on that skill being created\\n- Soft dependency: #2248 (Iron Law) \u2014 if the Iron Law finding format is adopted, `expected_findings` entries should align to the Symptom\u2192Source\u2192Consequence\u2192Remedy schema\\n- Blocks: #2253 (Health Score) \u2014 an eval baseline is the calibration input for severity-weighted deduction thresholds\\n- Blocks: Any CI eval gate for code-review skill changes (not yet planned)\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- Eval runner invocation method undecided: `run_code_review_evals.py` must call the code-reviewer agent. Three options \u2014 (a) spawn a `claude -p` subprocess per scenario (real LLM call, slow, costs tokens), (b) a deterministic rule-engine that applies skill patterns without an LLM call (fast, CI-safe, but requires a separate implementation), (c) use MCP artifact system to inject scenarios. This design decision is required before the runner script can be implemented.\\n- If #2250 (code-review-shared skill) directory is not created first, the target path `plugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/evals/` does not exist \u2014 the evals.json file has nowhere to land\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The schema and directory structure are well-specified. Scenario authoring for 10+ cases is straightforward once the format is settled. The main uncertainty is the runner design (see Blockers): if the runner uses `claude -p` subprocess the design is simple but expensive; a deterministic rule-engine approach is CI-safe but requires a separate Python implementation that mirrors skill rule logic. Resolving the runner design question converts this to Small effort for the eval corpus and a separate Small or Medium effort for the runner.\\n\\n### Current State\\n\\nNo eval suite exists inside `plugins/development-harness/`. The `code-reviewer` agent and all seven per-stack skills have zero test fixtures, no expected-output assertions, and no false-positive guard tests. A change to any `code-review-{stack}/SKILL.md` rule cannot be validated for accuracy or regression.\\n\\nConfirmed by command: `find plugins/development-harness -name \\\"evals.json\\\" -o -name \\\"evals\\\" -type d` returns empty.\\n\\nThe only existing eval precedent in this repo is `.agents/skills/copy-editing/evals/evals.json` (id, prompt, expected_output, assertions, files schema \u2014 confirmed by direct file read).\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/evals/evals.json` exists, modelled after `.agents/skills/copy-editing/evals/evals.json`. Each entry has:\\n\\n- `id` \u2014 sequential identifier\\n- `prompt` \u2014 code snippet or directory under review\\n- `expected_findings` \u2014 list of finding dimensions (e.g., `[\\\"error-handling.bare-except\\\", \\\"naming.magic-number\\\"]`) the reviewer MUST surface\\n- `false_positive_guards` \u2014 list of patterns the reviewer MUST NOT flag (e.g., a `try: ... except Exception: raise` block)\\n- `assertions` \u2014 verifiable claims about the produced report (e.g., \\\"Iron Law four-part structure present\\\", \\\"Source field cites a named principle\\\")\\n\\nA runner script `plugins/development-harness/scripts/run_code_review_evals.py` invokes the `code-reviewer` agent against each scenario and computes pass/fail. Initial scenario count: at least 10, covering each of the 7 universal quality dimensions plus at least one false-positive guard and one tradeoff scenario.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n```bash\\ntest -f plugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/evals/evals.json && \\\\\\n  uv run python -c \\\"import json; d = json.load(open('plugins/development-harness/skills/code-review-shared/evals/evals.json')); assert len(d['evals']) >= 10\\\"\\n```\\n\\nexits 0. Running `uv run plugins/development-harness/scripts/run_code_review_evals.py` reports a baseline pass rate that can be tracked across changes.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n```bash\\nfind plugins/development-harness -name \\\"evals.json\\\" -o -name \\\"evals\\\" -type d\\n```\\n\\nreturns nothing inside the development-harness plugin. The `code-reviewer` agent (`plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md`) and the seven per-stack skills (`code-review-python`, `code-review-typescript`, `code-review-nodejs`, `code-review-web`, `code-review-cli`, `code-review-llm`, `code-review-claude-skills`) have no test fixtures, no expected-output assertions, and no false-positive guard tests.\\n\\nThere is no way to detect whether a change to `code-review-python/SKILL.md` rules makes the code-reviewer more or less accurate. Compare with `.agents/skills/copy-editing/evals/evals.json` which already follows the eval pattern (id, prompt, expected_output, assertions, files) \u2014 the precedent exists locally for one skill but not for code-review.\\n\\nThe `agents/code-reviewer.md` agent is the central S6 Forensic Review enforcer for the entire SAM pipeline \u2014 every implementation passes through it before being marked complete. It has no regression test.\\n\\nbrooks-lint's evals.json contains 49 benchmark scenarios covering R1\u2013R6 production risks, T1\u2013T6 test risks, false-positive guards, and tradeoff edge cases. The benchmark is the forcing function that produces 94% pass rate vs 16% for plain LLM review (README.md lines 153\u2013166).\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"7030e2759a663a03006e4621d634206bb6b59baac6beafb43e08356a5ff73405\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPnLLng\"}"}